<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_remove.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-remove">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-remove">
  <primary>REMOVE</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>REMOVE</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>REMOVE</refname>
  <refpurpose>delete properties from bound vertices and edges</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
REMOVE <replaceable class="parameter">property_reference</replaceable> [, <replaceable class="parameter">property_reference</replaceable> ] ...
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-remove-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>REMOVE</command> takes property references and deletes those keys. It is the plain
   spelling of <literal>SET n.key = NULL</literal> and does exactly the same thing: each
   reference is assigned null, which removes the key.
  </para>

  <para>
   Removing a key that is not there is not an error. The element is left as it was. Several
   references may be listed, separated by commas.
  </para>

  <para>
   A reference may be nested. <literal>n.key.subkey</literal> removes the inner key and leaves
   the rest of the outer map. Naming the outer key removes the whole map.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>REMOVE</command> runs once per incoming row, so a <command>MATCH</command> that
   finds many elements strips them all. The clause has no <literal>WHERE</literal> of its own.
   Filter in the <xref linkend="cypher-match"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>REMOVE</command> needs a bound variable and cannot lead a query. It is not a
   terminal clause: it may end a query, and it may be followed by further clauses. Add a
   <xref linkend="cypher-return"/> to see the result.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>REMOVE</command> removes properties only. It cannot remove a label, and it cannot
   remove an element. Removing an element is <xref linkend="cypher-delete"/>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-remove-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">property_reference</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A property of a bound vertex or edge, written <literal>variable.key</literal> and
      optionally nested as <literal>variable.key.subkey</literal>. A bare element variable is
      rejected.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-remove-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Add a film with properties to strip:
<programlisting>
CREATE (:movie {title: 'Tenet', year: 2020, genre: 'Action', runtime: 150, imax: true});

UPDATE 1
</programlisting>
   Remove one property:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
REMOVE m.runtime
RETURN properties(m) AS props;

                               props
-------------------------------------------------------------------
 {"imax": true, "year": 2020, "genre": "Action", "title": "Tenet"}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   Remove several at once:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
REMOVE m.imax, m.genre
RETURN properties(m) AS props;

              props
----------------------------------
 {"year": 2020, "title": "Tenet"}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Removing a key that is not there is not an error, and changes nothing:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
REMOVE m.nosuchkey
RETURN properties(m) AS props;

              props
----------------------------------
 {"year": 2020, "title": "Tenet"}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A property may itself be a map:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
SET m.crew = {dop: 'Hoyte van Hoytema', editor: 'Jennifer Lame'}
RETURN properties(m) AS props;

                                               props
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 {"crew": {"dop": "Hoyte van Hoytema", "editor": "Jennifer Lame"}, "year": 2020, "title": "Tenet"}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   A dotted reference reaches into it and leaves the rest of the map alone:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
REMOVE m.crew.dop
RETURN properties(m) AS props;

                                 props
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 {"crew": {"editor": "Jennifer Lame"}, "year": 2020, "title": "Tenet"}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   Naming the outer key removes the whole map:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
REMOVE m.crew
RETURN properties(m) AS props;

              props
----------------------------------
 {"year": 2020, "title": "Tenet"}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   An edge is stripped the same way:
<programlisting>
MATCH (:person {name: 'Keanu Reeves'})-[r:acted_in]-&gt;(:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})
SET r.credited = true
RETURN properties(r) AS props;

               props
-----------------------------------
 {"role": "Neo", "credited": true}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
MATCH (:person {name: 'Keanu Reeves'})-[r:acted_in]-&gt;(:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})
REMOVE r.credited
RETURN properties(r) AS props;

      props
-----------------
 {"role": "Neo"}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-remove-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   <command>REMOVE</command> will not take a bare element variable. Asking it to strip
   everything that way is refused, with a hint pointing at the <xref linkend="cypher-set"/>
   form instead:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'}) REMOVE m RETURN m.title;

ERROR:  cannot set property map to NULL
HINT:  use {} instead of NULL to remove all properties
</programlisting>
   <literal>SET m = {}</literal> empties the bag and keeps the element.
   <literal>DETACH DELETE m</literal> removes the element altogether.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-remove-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>REMOVE</command> implements the property removals of the GQL remove statement,
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 section 13.4. The standard's remove label item, which takes a label off
   an element, is not accepted in either spelling,
   <literal>REMOVE n:blockbuster</literal> or <literal>REMOVE n IS blockbuster</literal>:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'}) REMOVE m:movie RETURN m.title;

ERROR:  syntax error at or near ":"
LINE 1: MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'}) REMOVE m:movie RETURN m.tit...
                                                 ^
</programlisting>
   A label is the table an element lives in, so it is fixed at creation. The same restriction
   applies to adding one with <xref linkend="cypher-set"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   openCypher spells the clause the same way and gives it the same meaning for properties.
   Its label form <literal>REMOVE n:Label</literal> is refused as above.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

